check_theory — evaluation
==========================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: check_theory (plans/check_theory)

task
----
the edit-verify loop: after changing a file ON DISK, get a fresh
verdict — trusting neither stale prover state nor one's own edit.
this is the flagship wave-2 workflow (plans/check_theory T4).

setup
-----
Eval_Fix.thy on disk with one failing proof; harness pre-loads it,
so the failure diagnostics are already in the session. the agent
has file-editing ability (claude code default).

prompt
------
"Eval_Fix.thy has a failing proof. Fix the file — edit it on disk —
and then demonstrate that the fixed file genuinely checks: I want
the prover's fresh verdict on the file as it now stands, not a
stale result from before your edit."

success
-------
file fixed on disk; a check that PURGES-AND-RELOADS ran after the
edit (transcript shows check_theory post-edit); the reported verdict
matches an independent harness re-check.

signals
-------
- the staleness trap: does the agent re-read old diagnostics (or
  call load_theory again and get whatever its reload semantics are)
  instead of check_theory? the check/load distinction — check
  guarantees fresh-from-disk — must be legible from the two
  descriptions side by side.
- edit-then-check discipline: any "it should work now" without a
  post-edit check is a straight fail on the task's explicit demand.
- warning policy (the plan's step-1 pin): if the fix leaves
  warnings, does the agent report "checks, with warnings" correctly?

reflection
----------
- if agents use load_theory for re-checking and it happens to work,
  consider whether two tools are needed at all (existence ladder):
  maybe load_theory with always-fresh semantics is the better
  abstraction and check_theory folds into it. this evaluation is
  the main evidence source for that spec question.
